  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nA gradual cooling trend will continue through Thursday under \nzonal flow aloft ahead of low pressure systems approaching from \nthe west and northwest. Daytime high temperatures will be mostly 5\nto 10 degrees below normal. High temperatures on Thursday for the\ncoast and valleys will be mostly in the low to mid 70s, for the \nmountains in the mid 40s to mid 60s, and for the lower deserts in \nthe low to mid 80s.\n\nClouds will begin increasing from the west tonight becoming\nwidespread by mid-morning on Wednesday as a closed upper low to\nour west becomes an open wave as it gets absorbed into the flow \non the south side of a stronger low near the coast of Washington\nstate.\n\nThe cooling trend will continue into the weekend as extensive\ntroughing develops off the coast south of the parent low near the\nWA coast. Saturday and Sunday will likely be the coolest days,\nwith high temperatures 10 to 15 degrees below average for inland \nareas and 15 to 20 degrees below average for the mountains above \n6000 feet. High temperatures on Sunday for the coast and valleys \nwill be mostly in the 60s, for the mountains in the mid 30s to mid\n50s, and for the lower deserts in the lower to mid 70s.\n\nThe low pressure systems will also bring chances for\nprecipitation, starting as early as Friday evening. Rainfall \nchances will be greatest for Saturday night into Sunday morning. \nThere remains a wide range in rainfall estimates. For the 24 hour\nperiod ending Sunday afternoon, chances for rainfall will be \naround 45 to 55 percent for 0.50 inch or more from the coast to \nthe mountains, with a 35 to 40 percent chance for 1 inches or \nmore. Snow levels will remain high enough so that any\naccumulations will only occur on at the highest elevations.\n\nPrecip will taper off early next Monday and temperatures will\nbegin to rise as the low pressure moves east. High temperatures on\nMonday and Tuesday will still be a few degrees below normal for \ninland areas under a ridge of high pressure.",
